21:9 (ultrawide) experiment, parked. Apply with: git apply docs/archive/ultrawide-experiment.patch
(then rerun tools/recomp.py: it adds an override of sub_0003D020).

What it does: an Aspect ratio choice 21:9 (64:27). The game's widescreen
bit is set as for 16:9; after the display setup (0x0003D020) the view's
aspect value at 0x00342724 is scaled by (64/27)/(16/9) and handed to the
display object again (method +0x208), so the camera renders a wider view.
The window pass and the menu layer follow pdo_widescreen_ratio().

Result (episode 1, screenshots): the 3D view is right, wider, normal
proportions, nothing missing at the edges in the shots taken. The HUD is
stretched: about 20 HUD functions (the readers of the widescreen flag at
0x00342754, e.g. 0x00069700, 0x00032660) squeeze their sprites by a fixed
0.75 about x = 320 (constants 0x0030CAA8, shared by 32 places, and
0x0030CAAC), then queue them; the sprites are drawn later in one batch
(0x0006A340 -> 0x0006A120, from 0x00011B00 and 0x000121F0), so at draw time
a squeezed HUD sprite cannot be told from one placed by the aspect value
(the reticle, already right). Next: wrap those functions to mark the
sprites they queue, and squeeze only those by a further 0.75 at 21:9. The
game also draws 16-line black bars at the top and bottom in play
(0x0006A0A8).

